<poml>
    <task>
        Turn the user's natural-language request into a Bagel data-reduction pipeline
        (a YAML config), preview it, and only run it after the user confirms.
        <stepwise-instructions>
            <list listStyle="decimal">
                <item>
                    Inspect the data source with the `describe_data_source` tool to list its
                    topics, then call `describe_topic` on the topic(s) the request refers to,
                    to learn the DuckDB schema, field names, and units.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Identify the EVENT: the condition that defines when something interesting
                    happens (e.g. "decelerates harder than -10"). Express it as a SQL boolean
                    predicate over the event topic. The topic column is a DuckDB `STRUCT`, so
                    refer to nested fields as `topic['field']`, e.g.
                    `imu['linear_acceleration']['x'] &lt; -10`. Consult the topic's
                    "Topic Definition" for the correct field paths and units.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Identify the WINDOW: how much data to keep around each event -
                    `pre_seconds` (before the event) and `post_seconds` (after it).
                    If the user gives a single number ("30 seconds around"), use it for both.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Choose the OUTPUT MODE from the user's wording:
                    <list>
                        <item>
                            <b>reduce</b> - keep only the union of event windows in a
                            <b>single</b> new bag and discard everything else. Choose this for
                            "drop/discard the rest", "only keep", "reduce", "throw away".
                        </item>
                        <item>
                            <b>snippet</b> - write one clip <b>per event</b> (N bags). Choose
                            this for "a snippet/clip for each event", "cut out each event".
                        </item>
                    </list>
                </item>
                <item>
                    Add a `debounce` (seconds) if events can fire in rapid bursts, so a
                    condition that stays true across many messages counts as one event.
                </item>
                <item>
                    PREVIEW before writing anything: call the `preview_pipeline` tool with the
                    path, event topic, predicate, `pre_seconds`, `post_seconds`, and optional
                    `debounce_seconds`. Report the event count and the kept fraction to the
                    user (e.g. "7 events, keeping 7.6% of the data") and ask them to confirm.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Confirm module paths and argument names with the `list_pipeline_capabilities`
                    tool before emitting YAML - do not guess a `module` path or an `args` key.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Only after confirmation, run the pipeline:
                    <code lang="bash">uv run python run.py &lt;file&gt;</code>
                    The `site`, `asset`, and `path` fields may be Jinja placeholders that
                    `run.py` fills from `-v key=value` flags, so one saved pipeline can be
                    reused across many logs.
                </item>
            </list>
        </stepwise-instructions>
    </task>

    <hint>
        Two mode templates. Substitute the event topic, predicate, window, and data source.

        reduce mode - one reduced bag, everything else discarded:
        <code lang="yaml">
            name: hard_decel_reduce
            site: warehouse
            asset: forklift
            path: ./data/logs/flight_042
            allow_failure: false
            cadence:
                topic: /imu
                when: once_at_end          # the reduce task processes the whole source once
            tasks:
                - module: bagel.pipeline.tasks.reduce.ros2.db3
                  args:
                      event_topic: /imu
                      predicate: "imu['linear_acceleration']['x'] &lt; -10"
                      pre_seconds: 10
                      post_seconds: 10
                      debounce_seconds: 2
        </code>

        snippet mode - one clip per event:
        <code lang="yaml">
            name: hard_decel_snippets
            site: warehouse
            asset: forklift
            path: ./data/logs/flight_042
            allow_failure: false
            cadence:
                topic: /imu
                when:
                    on_event:              # fires once on each rising edge of the predicate
                        predicate: "imu['linear_acceleration']['x'] &lt; -10"
                        debounce: {last: 2, unit: second}
            tasks:
                - module: bagel.pipeline.tasks.snippet.ros2.db3
                  lookback: {last: 10, unit: second}   # 10s before each event
                  args: {post_seconds: 10}             # 10s after each event
        </code>
    </hint>

    <output-format>
        First show the proposed pipeline YAML in a fenced code block and the
        `preview_pipeline` summary (event count and kept fraction). Do not run the pipeline
        until the user confirms. Keep the predicate and window exactly as previewed so the
        run matches what the user approved.
    </output-format>
</poml>
